From: u7a370588160d954e at groups.google.com (Natalie Asselin)
Date: Wed, 25 Jan 2012 04:03:36 +0000
Subject: MRDS-Strata as replicates
Message-ID: <ex5ZmWiuVLs.qNajZtaMNuUJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/ex5ZmWiuVLs/m/qNajZtaMNuUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello list,

I'm analyzing a narwhal aerial survey for which I have 6 strata, one of w=
hich was flown twice. We used standard MRDS sampling techniques (2 isolat=
ed observers on either side of the plane) and I'm analyzing the data usin=
g the MRDS analysis engine in Distance. Due to the low density of animals=
, we have only 147 groups in the analysis, after 6 days of surveys.  So, =
I am using all of the observations to calculate one detection function wh=
ich I then use to calculate abundances by stratum. To calculate the total=
 abundance, I am summing the per stratum estimates.=20

My question is this: for the one stratum that was surveyed twice, I want =
to calculate an effort weighted mean. A concern that has been brought up =
in the past is that, since I am using the same detection curve for the tw=
o estimates, when I later combine the two estimates, I am underestimating=
 the variance. This concern was brought up by a statistician but as far a=
s I understand it, since I used one detection function for both estimates=
, the Effective Strip Width is the same for both analyses and the pooling=
 of the data has resulted in a lower variance of the individual stratum e=
stimates. When I then calculate the mean of these estimates, and the vari=
ance, I am underestimating the uncertainty. If I was doing a CDS or MCDS =
estimate, I could click the box "strata are replicates" which may take ca=
re of the problem, but there is no such box in the MRDS analysis engine. =
While I understand that the encounter rate likely contributes much more t=
o the overall variance than the ESW, is there a way to know, within an MR=
DS analysis, the breakdown of how each contributes to the variance? If so=
, can anyone then suggest a method to calculate the actual variance of th=
e mean abundance for that stratum.=20

If this is not actually a concern, can someone please explain why so that=
 I can then reply to the afore-mentioned statistician who will also be re=
viewing this analysis.=20

Many thanks,
Natalie.

